{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\n\n`src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/dispatch_one.py` measures **796 lines** against the repository's\nhard 800-line module-size gate (`.github/module-size-limit.txt`), which CI enforces with no allowlist\nescape hatch. Four lines of headroom is not enough for the sibling story that makes the outbound\ncreate path target a per-ticket project, so that story cannot land until this file has room.\n\n## Approach\n\nExtract the entire `update_one` sub-tree — lines **432-796** — into a new\n`dispatch_update.py`. This is a pre-existing call-graph seam, not a mechanical cut: `update_one`\n(`:447`) calls `_update_one_apply_parent` (`:497`), `_update_one_scalar_update` (`:500`),\n`_update_one_dispatch_labels` (`:506`) and `_update_one_dispatch_links` (`:510`), and\n`_update_one_scalar_update` is the sole caller of `_is_illegal_transition_400`. Nothing above line\n432 calls into the block; the only dependency runs downward, through `_call_with_retry` (`:71-143`),\nwhich stays put.\n\nThe repository has already made this exact split twice: `dispatch_apply_phases.py` (226 lines)\nholds three other `update_one` phases, and `batch_dispatch.py` (148 lines) is the stable re-export\nfacade. This extraction follows the pattern rather than inventing one.\n\n**The import edge the new module must carry.** `update_one` does not only call the four helpers that\nmove with it — it also calls `_update_one_apply_reporter` (`:498`), `_update_one_filter_fields`\n(`:499`) and `_update_one_dispatch_comments` (`:507`), which already live in `dispatch_apply_phases`\nand are imported at `dispatch_one.py:53-55`. Those three imports move to `dispatch_update.py` with\ntheir caller; `dispatch_one` drops them, since nothing left in it uses them. This is what makes the\nextraction land in the module that already owns the other `update_one` phases.\n\n**The one hazard, and how it is avoided.** `dispatch_one.py` must **not** re-export `update_one`\nback for convenience — that would create `dispatch_one → dispatch_update → dispatch_one`, because\n`dispatch_update` imports `_call_with_retry` from `dispatch_one`. Instead `batch_dispatch.py`'s\nimport block (`:31-40`) is re-pointed at `dispatch_update` for `update_one` and\n`_is_illegal_transition_400`. That is the only production re-point needed: `applier.py:122-130`\nalready imports both symbols **from `batch_dispatch`**, not from `dispatch_one`, so its `__all__` at\n`:329` keeps resolving with no edit. `batch_dispatch` is the established public surface and continues\nto import `_call_with_retry`, `create_one` and `delete_one` from `dispatch_one` (`:31`), so the\nimport direction stays acyclic.\n\n**One consumer does need an edit.** `tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/apply/test_synced_fields_reporting_e6e9.py:36`\nimports `update_one` **directly** from `dispatch_one`, so it breaks the moment the symbol moves and\nthe no-re-export rule forbids papering over it. That import is re-pointed at `batch_dispatch`, which\nis where every other consumer already gets it. This is the single test edit this story makes, and it\nis enumerated rather than discovered during implementation — an earlier draft of this plan wrongly\nclaimed no test changes were needed.\n\nThis is a behaviour-preserving move: no logic is rewritten.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/dispatch_update.py` (new) — the moved sub-tree, ~405 lines\n- `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/dispatch_one.py` — drops to ~431 lines\n- `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/batch_dispatch.py` — import block re-pointed; the only\n  production re-point\n- `tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/apply/test_synced_fields_reporting_e6e9.py` — its direct\n  `from ... dispatch_one import update_one` at `:36` re-pointed at `batch_dispatch`\n\n`applier.py` is deliberately **not** in scope: it already imports the moved symbols from\n`batch_dispatch` (`:122-130`), so it needs no change.\n\n## Testing\n\nNo new behaviour, so no new behavioural tests: the oracle is the existing suite passing **unchanged**,\nwhich is what proves the move preserved behaviour. Two specific hazards are verified rather than\nassumed:\n\n- Monkeypatch survival: there are **five** module-attribute patch sites on `dispatch_one`, across\n  three test modules — `apply/test_applier_identity_write.py:156,195`,\n  `test_cloud_rate_limit_sweep_heldout.py:258,279` and `test_error_taxonomy.py:61` — and every one of\n  them patches the same attribute, `dispatch_one.time`. All five stay safe: `time` is consumed by\n  `_call_with_retry` and `create_one`, both of which remain resident, and `dispatch_update` reaches\n  the retry helper by importing `_call_with_retry` from `dispatch_one`, so the sleep call still\n  resolves against `dispatch_one`'s globals where the patch is applied. Confirm all five pass\n  unedited — this is the assertion that proves the split did not silently defeat a patch.\n- Import-cycle check: importing `dispatch_one` and `dispatch_update` in either order must succeed,\n  asserted directly so a future re-export cannot silently reintroduce the cycle.\n- Direct-importer coverage: the one re-pointed test\n  (`test_synced_fields_reporting_e6e9.py`) passes after its import is changed, and a repository-wide\n  grep for `from rebar_reconciler.dispatch_one import update_one` returns no remaining hit. The grep\n  is part of the oracle because this consumer was missed by symbol-level analysis and only a string\n  search surfaced it.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] `dispatch_one.py` and `dispatch_update.py` are each at or below 650 lines; checked by running\n      the module-size gate and recording both counts.\n- [ ] The full existing test suite passes, with exactly one existing test file edited —\n      `tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/apply/test_synced_fields_reporting_e6e9.py`, whose direct import\n      is re-pointed — and one file added,\n      `tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/test_dispatch_update_split.py`, which holds this story's\n      structural assertions; checked by the gating test command plus confirming the diff under\n      `tests/` touches only those two paths.\n- [ ] All five `dispatch_one.time` patch sites pass unedited; checked by running the three named\n      test modules.\n- [ ] `dispatch_update.py` imports `_update_one_apply_reporter`, `_update_one_filter_fields` and\n      `_update_one_dispatch_comments` from `dispatch_apply_phases`, and `dispatch_one.py` no longer\n      does; checked by asserting the import lists of both modules.\n- [ ] No `from rebar_reconciler.dispatch_one import update_one` remains anywhere in the tree; checked\n      by a repository-wide string grep returning zero hits.\n- [ ] `dispatch_one.py` does not import from `dispatch_update.py`; checked by a test that imports the\n      two modules in both orders and asserts no `ImportError`.\n- [ ] `update_one` and `_is_illegal_transition_400` remain reachable through `batch_dispatch` **and**\n      through `applier`'s existing re-export; checked by asserting both resolve from each module\n      after the move, which confirms `applier.py` needed no edit.\n- [ ] No behaviour change: checked by confirming the moved functions' bodies are identical to their\n      pre-move text apart from import lines."}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1786164022792334001,"uuid":"81011803-4f38-4f5d-a9d2-695bd08aef1c"}